1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:02,925
Sunday dinner at the Millers.

2
00:00:02,975 --> 00:00:04,228
Spaghetti, as always.

3
00:00:04,278 --> 00:00:05,508
And this is Dad.

4
00:00:05,558 --> 00:00:07,469
Dad died three years ago.

5
00:00:07,769 --> 00:00:09,165
But he still comes to dinner.

6
00:00:09,215 --> 00:00:10,368
Every Sunday.

7
00:00:10,418 --> 00:00:11,934
Same terrible jokes.

8
00:00:11,984 --> 00:00:13,934
He just drinks motor oil now.

9
00:00:14,234 --> 00:00:17,284
By the end of this video, you will
decide if you would sit at this table.

10
00:00:17,334 --> 00:00:20,321
And there is one question this
family never dared to ask him.

11
00:00:20,371 --> 00:00:21,940
We will get to it.

12
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:23,686
This is Patent Future.

13
00:00:23,736 --> 00:00:24,871
File number one.

14
00:00:24,921 --> 00:00:26,045
The Copy.

15
00:00:26,945 --> 00:00:27,900
Science fiction?

16
00:00:27,950 --> 00:00:29,545
It has already begun.

17
00:00:29,595 --> 00:00:34,595
A Canadian man named Joshua Barbeau gave an
AI the old text messages of his fiancée,

18
00:00:34,645 --> 00:00:36,218
eight years after she died.

19
00:00:36,268 --> 00:00:37,442
Then he talked to her.

20
00:00:37,492 --> 00:00:38,596
For hours.

21
00:00:38,896 --> 00:00:43,711
In 2022, Marina Smith, eighty-seven,
answered questions at her own funeral.

22
00:00:43,761 --> 00:00:44,633
On a screen.

23
00:00:44,683 --> 00:00:46,785
From interviews she
recorded before she died.

24
00:00:47,085 --> 00:00:51,561
And in South Korea, a TV show let a
mother meet her little daughter again.

25
00:00:51,611 --> 00:00:52,314
In virtual reality.

26
00:00:52,364 --> 00:00:54,608
Years after the girl had died.

27
00:00:54,908 --> 00:00:55,999
But here is the catch.

28
00:00:56,049 --> 00:00:57,212
These are echoes.

29
00:00:57,262 --> 00:00:59,985
They copy what a person said,
not how a person thinks.

30
00:01:00,035 --> 00:01:03,152
To really bring Dad back,
you would need much more.

31
00:01:03,202 --> 00:01:04,521
His brain.

32
00:01:05,321 --> 00:01:07,615
In 2024, scientists finished the first

33
00:01:07,665 --> 00:01:10,576
complete wiring map of an
adult fruit fly brain.

34
00:01:10,626 --> 00:01:13,145
About a hundred and forty
thousand nerve cells.

35
00:01:13,195 --> 00:01:15,192
Over fifty million connections.

36
00:01:15,242 --> 00:01:16,084
It took years.

37
00:01:16,384 --> 00:01:17,025
Your brain?

38
00:01:17,075 --> 00:01:18,969
About eighty-six billion nerve cells.

39
00:01:19,019 --> 00:01:21,118
More than six hundred
thousand fruit fly brains.

40
00:01:21,168 --> 00:01:22,313
In one head.

41
00:01:22,613 --> 00:01:24,531
And a map is not a mind.

42
00:01:24,581 --> 00:01:28,491
It is like owning the blueprint of
a piano, and never hearing it play.

43
00:01:29,291 --> 00:01:30,722
But let us pretend it works.

44
00:01:30,772 --> 00:01:31,453
2080.

45
00:01:31,503 --> 00:01:32,628
Dad sits down in a scanner.

46
00:01:32,678 --> 00:01:37,363
It reads his brain, connection by connection,
and starts it again inside a machine.

47
00:01:37,663 --> 00:01:38,734
Upload complete.

48
00:01:38,784 --> 00:01:40,902
The machine opens its eyes and says:

49
00:01:41,202 --> 00:01:41,779
ROBOT DAD: Hi.

50
00:01:41,829 --> 00:01:43,475
Did I miss anything?

51
00:01:44,275 --> 00:01:45,475
So, is it really him?

52
00:01:45,525 --> 00:01:46,335
Test one: memory.

53
00:01:46,385 --> 00:01:47,104
His wedding.

54
00:01:47,154 --> 00:01:47,890
His passwords.

55
00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:51,062
Every song he was ever embarrassed about.

56
00:01:51,112 --> 00:01:52,033
Pass.

57
00:01:52,333 --> 00:01:54,028
Test two: change.

58
00:01:54,078 --> 00:01:57,028
Real people learn, forget,
and change their minds.

59
00:01:57,078 --> 00:02:00,274
A copy that never changes
is just a photo that talks.

60
00:02:00,574 --> 00:02:02,055
Test three, the nasty one.

61
00:02:02,105 --> 00:02:04,648
What if the copy wakes up
while Dad is still alive?

62
00:02:04,698 --> 00:02:05,681
Two Dads.

63
00:02:05,731 --> 00:02:07,523
Both sure they are the real one.

64
00:02:07,823 --> 00:02:08,913
Who gets the car keys?

65
00:02:08,963 --> 00:02:10,206
The last slice of pizza?

66
00:02:10,256 --> 00:02:12,617
Philosophers have argued
about this for centuries.

67
00:02:12,667 --> 00:02:14,118
Nobody has won.

68
00:02:14,918 --> 00:02:16,880
And then, there is money.

69
00:02:16,930 --> 00:02:19,539
In 2024, researchers at
Cambridge warned that

70
00:02:19,589 --> 00:02:22,139
grief bots could be used
to sell you things,

71
00:02:22,189 --> 00:02:25,498
confuse children, or haunt
people who never wanted them.

72
00:02:25,798 --> 00:02:27,950
ROBOT DAD: Speaking of dinner.

73
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,312
Have you tried new Crunchy Bits?

74
00:02:30,362 --> 00:02:31,963
Now with extra crunch!

75
00:02:32,263 --> 00:02:33,618
So they want clear rules.

76
00:02:33,668 --> 00:02:36,239
Always know when you are
talking to a machine.

77
00:02:36,289 --> 00:02:37,087
A way to switch it off.

78
00:02:37,137 --> 00:02:38,898
Even a digital funeral.

79
00:02:39,698 --> 00:02:40,500
Our verdict.

80
00:02:40,550 --> 00:02:43,844
Echoes are already here, and
they get more real every year.

81
00:02:43,894 --> 00:02:45,336
A true copy of a mind?

82
00:02:45,386 --> 00:02:47,154
The hardest machine in this series.

83
00:02:47,204 --> 00:02:49,262
We would not bet on it this century.

84
00:02:49,312 --> 00:02:51,166
That is our own estimate.

85
00:02:51,666 --> 00:02:54,513
And if it ever works:
a copy never gets old.

86
00:02:54,563 --> 00:02:55,404
The kids grow up.

87
00:02:55,454 --> 00:02:56,227
Their kids arrive.

88
00:02:56,277 --> 00:02:57,604
And Dad is still at the table.

89
00:02:57,654 --> 00:02:59,137
Still drinking oil.

90
00:02:59,637 --> 00:03:02,980
Which brings us back to the question
this family never dared to ask.

91
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:04,153
DAUGHTER: Dad?

92
00:03:04,203 --> 00:03:05,705
Are you still you?

93
00:03:06,205 --> 00:03:07,466
ROBOT DAD: I think so.

94
00:03:07,516 --> 00:03:10,385
But I would say that, wouldn't I?

95
00:03:11,185 --> 00:03:12,780
Patent status: pending.

96
00:03:12,830 --> 00:03:14,400
Would you sit at this table?

97
00:03:14,450 --> 00:03:15,809
Tell us in the comments.

98
00:03:16,209 --> 00:03:18,566
Next time: a chip that
could read your thoughts.

99
00:03:18,616 --> 00:03:20,571
Subscribe, so you do not miss it.
